A long time ago, in a dungeon far, far away, in the Principality of
the Mists, a group of wizards created a game called rogue. 
Wizards with names like Ken Arnold and Michael Toy and Yendor-spelled-backwards.  
Berzerk wizards, famous for their curses.  Wizards who in even
earlier days had walked down the halls and had stone knives thrown
at them by nasty little dwarves.  Wizards who wanted to ruin the
best minds of a generation, enslaving them to long nights staring at
the fires in little boxes with braindamaged keyboards.

They succeeded, and the plague of rogue spread across the nets like
wildfire, and grew, and evolved, until the net.gods, staggering
under its constant reappearance, banished it for a while.*

In a later age, the desire to bash dragons emerged again, at a place
where bashing dragons may have been frowned upon, but hacking was
viewed as a legitimate use of resources.  And the hack was hacked,
and spread out across the networks, until it was perverted into what
we see today.  At this point you may want to type V to nethack, to
read the more recent histories.

[* actually, it was kind of jointly acknowledged that rogue source
was big, and there were too many private copies around eating disk
space, and where rogue was installed in /usr/games it was normally
in the top-10 CPU-hogs list, and that if you had a copy of it, the
nice thing to do for the other administrators on the net might be to
not post it; let them install it if they could handle the load.
But the net was younger and smaller and more cooperative in those
days, before its immanent death had been predicted.... ]

So, anyway, this is what rogue looked like then, before doors could
open and close, and little dogs had not yet been tamed, and a
two-handed sword was the best weapon around.

		So now you're as smart as Ken Arnold ...

